# THE LAYERS THAT DENY HAVING A REMAINDER AT ALL. One row per layer filing the second branch
# of the `StatedRemainder` wrapper -- a typed reason there is no remainder, and the note that
# argues for it.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS THE MODEL BEING CONTRADICTED IN ITS OWN FORMAT, WHICH IS WHAT THE WRAPPER IS
#    FOR. The model claims every layer has a remainder. Requiring the wrapper forces a sender
#    who disagrees to say so explicitly rather than leaving a field empty. Every row here is
#    somebody exercising that right.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND THE RIGHT IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS, WHICH IS WHY THIS ARM IS A `ClaimAbsence`.
#    "There is no remainder" reads two ways: nothing to subtract from, or the difference is
#    zero. Only the first is an absence. A difference of zero is a CLEARANCE -- `Fit` settles
#    the line-to-line case in prose, "minimum clearance is zero" -- carrying a quantity of
#    [0, 0, 0] and a sign, and an absence throws the sign away. So `none` is not spellable
#    here, and a layer that means "the two coincide exactly" files the clearance.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND FOR TWO REVISIONS THESE ROWS WERE INVISIBLE. `ingest.sql` read only the first branch,
#    so a denial arrived as five NULLs -- sign, absorber and quantity all blank -- which is
#    exactly the shape of a document that said nothing. One of the two notes says so in as many
#    words: "filed as a counter-example ... NOT AS A GAP IN THIS DOCUMENT". A gap is what was
#    stored. The note is carried here because keeping only the reason keeps the fact and loses
#    the argument.
#
# ⭐ RUN THIS ALONE AND READ THE NOTES. Two rows, and both now say the same thing in different
#   words: this layer states no nameplate, so `r = n - d` has no `n` and the question is
#   malformed rather than answered with nothing. ⚠️ NEITHER MAY SAY "a supply with no quantum
#   divides exactly", which is a different claim and a false one: no quantum kills the RESIDUE
#   term of `r = m*q - (demand mod q)` and leaves `n - d` standing. Let the notes disagree with
#   the structure and the rule downstream prints the right reason
#   ("no nameplate is stated") beside the wrong one for two revisions.
-- pm:Layer/pm:remainder taking the pm:absent branch of pm:StatedRemainder.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.remainder_absent      AS reason,
       l.remainder_absent_note AS argument
FROM pm.layer l
WHERE l.remainder_absent IS NOT NULL
